 to Reality","covers":[10551604],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL21692755W"}],"key":"/books/OL31873705M","identifiers":{},"classifications":{},"isbn_10":["0735223793"],"isbn_13":["9780735223790"],"weight":"418 grams","table_of_contents":[{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Contents","pagenum":"ix","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Preface: Born Again","pagenum":"xi","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Introduction","pagenum":"1","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"I","title":"What There Is","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"1","title":"There's Plenty of Space","pagenum":"13","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"2","title":"There's Plenty of Time","pagenum":"41","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"3","title":"There Are Very Few Ingredients","pagenum":"61","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"4","title":"There Are Very Few Laws","pagenum":"93","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"5","title":"There's Plenty of Matter and Energy","pagenum":"126","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"II","title":"Beginnings and Ends","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"6","title":"Cosmic History Is an Open Book","pagenum":"145","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"7","title":"Complexity Emerges","pagenum":"160","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"8","title":"There's Plenty More to See","pagenum":"168","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"9","title":"Mysteries Remain","pagenum":"187","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"10","title":"Complementarity Is Mind-Expanding","pagenum":"206","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Afterword: The Long Voyage Home","pagenum":"223","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Acknowledgments","pagenum":"229","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Appendix","pagenum":"231","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Index","pagenum":"243","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"publish_places":["New York, USA"],"first_sentence":{"type":"/type/text","value":"The Universe is a strange place."},"copyright_date":"2021","pagination":"xviii, 254p","lc_classifications":["QC6.4.R42W55 2021","QC6.4.R42 W55 2021"],"local_id":["urn:bwbsku:W7-DIV-968"],"lccn":["2020020086"],"oclc_numbers":["1153517384"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Nobel laureate Wilczek presents ten insights that illuminate what the world is and how it works. Fundamentals is built around a simple but profound idea: the models of the world we construct as children are practical and adequate for everyday life, but they do not bring in the surprising and mind-expanding revelations of modern science. To do that, we must look at the world anew, combining clear thinking with an openness to wonder. This \"born again\" world is in many ways larger, fuller, and much stranger than it appears. Through an exploration of space, time, matter, and ideas--and equipped with facts, questions, and speculations--Wilczek guides us through the past, present, and future of fundamental science.\n\n\"One of our great contemporary scientists presents ten insights that illuminate what every thinking person needs to know about what the world is and how it works. Nobel Prize winner Frank Wilczek's Fundamentals is built around a simple but profound idea: the models of the world we construct as children are practical and adequate for everyday life, but they do not bring in the surprising and mind-expanding revelations of modern science. To do that, we must look at the world anew, combining clear thinking with an openness to wonder. This \"born again\" world is in many ways larger, fuller, and much stranger than it appears. Through an exploration of space, time, matter, and ideas--and equipped with facts, questions, and brilliant speculations--Wilczek guides us through the past, present, and future of fundamental science. Readers will emerge with an expanded vision of our universe. Author Bio: Frank Wilczek won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2004 for work he did as a graduate student. He was among the earliest MacArthur fellows, and has won many awards both for his scientific work and his writing. He is the author of A Beautiful Question, The Lightness of Being, Fantastic Realities, Longing for the Harmonies, and hundreds of articles in leading scientific journals. His \"Wilczek's Universe\" column appears regularly in the Wall Street Journal. Wilczek is the Herman Feshbach Professor of Physics at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, founding director of the T. D. Lee Institute and chief scientist at the Wilczek Quantum Center in Shanghai, China, and a distinguished professor at Arizona State University and Stockholm University\"--"},"latest_revision":6,"revision":6,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2021-01-19T21:41:00.593508"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-02-23T10:27:04.368318"}}},"OLID:OL33886659M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL33886659M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL33886659M/Fundamentals","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL33886659M/Fundamentals","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/11998435-S.jpg","details":{"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"title":"Fundamentals","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL894144A","name":"Frank Wilczek"}],"publish_date":"Jan 11, 2022","source_records":["amazon:0735223904","bwb:9780735223905"],"number_of_pages":272,"publishers":["Penguin Books"],"isbn_10":["0735223904"],"isbn_13":["9780735223905"],"physical_format":"paperback","full_title":"Fundamentals Ten Keys to Reality","subtitle":"Ten Keys to Reality","notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Source title: Fundamentals: Ten Keys to Reality"},"covers":[11998435],"works":[{"key":"/works/O